Let $(X,\|\cdot\|)$ be an $F$-space, that is, a real topological vector space whose topology is induced by an $F$-norm $\|\cdot\|:X\to[0,\infty)$ satisfying:
(i) $\|x\|=0$ iff $x=0$;
(ii) $\|\alpha x\|=\|x\|$ for every scalar $\alpha$ with $|\alpha|=1$;
(iii) $\|x+y\|\le\|x\|+\|y\|$;
(iv) $\|\alpha_n x\|\to 0$ whenever $\alpha_n\to 0$ in scalars, and $\|\alpha x_n\|\to 0$ whenever $\|x_n\|\to 0$;
and such that $X$ is complete with respect to the metric $d(x,y)=\|x-y\|$.

Define the rotation group of $\|\cdot\|$ by
$$G(\|\cdot\|):=\{T:X\to X\mid T\text{ is a linear bijection with }\|Tx\|=\|x\|\text{ for all }x\in X\}.$$

A norm $\|\cdot\|'$ on a real vector space $Y$ is called homogeneous if $\|\alpha y\|'=|\alpha|\,\|y\|'$ for every scalar $\alpha$ and every $y\in Y$ (i.e. an ordinary Banach-space norm).

Question: Given an $F$-space $(X,\|\cdot\|)$, does there always exist a Banach space $(Y,\|\cdot\|')$ with a homogeneous norm such that the rotation group $G(\|\cdot\|')$ of $(Y,\|\cdot\|')$ is isomorphic (as an abstract group, via a group isomorphism implementing the action) to $G(\|\cdot\|)$, i.e. such that $G(\|\cdot\|')=G(\|\cdot\|)$?

